{"kind":"request","text":"Close the measurement gaps that stopped this evidence being measured rather than extrapolated.","body":{"conditions":"Discovered while executing the parent request at main 316d5aeb. Five gaps in internal/perfscenario, internal/perflane and cmd/gitseq-perf: (1) the gitseq.performance/v1 depth axis stops at 100,000, below the FIRST-PRODUCTION envelope of 500,000; (2) actor count is not an axis at all, every fixture has exactly one actor 'operator', so actor-count cost had to be measured with synthetic logs; (3) dependency fan-out is not an axis, so fan-out cost also had to be measured synthetically; (4) the contract requires git_process_count and git_duration_ns but results never populate them, so the subprocess figures came from a PATH shim; (5) fixture Materialize copies .git/gitseq including the checkpoint pointer, so tail=-1 does not produce a cold run — at depth 1,000 the pointer reselected the depth-257 checkpoint, honest_fallback failed its own correctness assertion with 'fallback source = verified_signed_checkpoint_tail, want verified_cold_full_audit', and no depth-1,000 cold-audit number could be recorded. Fixture generation also costs about 129 ms per record, so the contract's own 100,000 tier needs about 3.6 h to build. Satisfied when actor count and fan-out are contract axes, the depth axis reaches the FIRST-PRODUCTION envelope, results carry the two required git metrics, and requesting no checkpoint reliably yields a cold run.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}